Scaling test-time computation has emerged as an alternative to parameter scaling. \citet{snell2025scaling} show that optimally allocated test-time compute can outperform larger models and establish a framework for analyzing the compute-accuracy tradeoff at inference. Reasoning-specialized models such as DeepSeek-R1 \citep{guo2025deepseekr1} internalize extended chain-of-thought through reinforcement learning and produce longer but more coherent traces. Diversity-aware training methods explicitly optimize for trace diversity during test-time scaling \citep{diverseaware2026}. These developments interact with our findings through the $C_0$ diagnostic. Reasoning models produce highly consistent outputs across samples: QwQ-32B achieves $C_0 = 2.6\%$ on our benchmarks, while DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B ($C_0 = 58.3\%$) behaves as an instruction-scale model rather than a reasoning model, possibly because its 7B capacity is insufficient to sustain the deterministic reasoning chains learned through distillation. When nearly all traces converge to the same answer, any aggregation method reduces to the unanimous output. The $C_0$ metric thus provides a zero-cost signal for practitioners to determine whether post-hoc aggregation can offer any benefit for a given model.

\paragraph{Verification and Reranking.}
Trained verifiers offer an alternative path to improving multi-path reasoning. Outcome-level reward models (ORMs) score complete solutions and select the highest-ranked candidate \citep{cobbe2021gsm8k}. Process reward models (PRMs) assign step-level scores and enable finer-grained selection \citep{lightman2024prm}. Math-Shepherd automates process supervision annotation and removes the need for human labels \citep{wang2024mathshepherd}. V-STaR extends self-improvement by training verifiers on both correct and incorrect model-generated solutions \citep{hosseini2024vstar}. Reasoning-aware variants score individual reasoning paths rather than training separate verifiers: RASC assigns confidence scores based on textual quality indicators and consistency \citep{wan2024rasc}, while ReASC frames adaptive sampling as evidence sufficiency and aggregates response-level confidence \citep{kim2026reasc}. These approaches share SICA's goal of selecting better answers from a candidate pool but differ in that they either train a separate scoring model or use lightweight heuristics grounded in textual features rather than formal logic. SICA's constraint extraction requires no training data and no additional model; it relies entirely on the reasoning model's own ability to formalize its logic. Our results suggest that this self-reliance is precisely the problem. Without an external signal to ground constraint quality, self-extracted constraints encode the same biases present in the original traces and fail to provide corrective information.

\paragraph{Constraint Satisfaction in NLP.}
Constraint-based formulations have gained traction for evaluating and improving LLM reasoning. SATBench benchmarks LLMs on Boolean satisfiability problems and reveals systematic weaknesses in logical structure handling \citep{satbench2025}. Work on integrating LLMs with constraint propagation has explored constrained text generation and hybrid solver architectures \citep{llmmeets_cp2025}. SICA's MAX-SAT formulation sits within this broader trend: it uses Z3 to find the maximum-weight satisfiable subset of self-extracted constraints. The key difference is that SICA's constraints originate from the model's own reasoning traces rather than from problem specifications or external knowledge bases. Concurrent work confirms these limitations from complementary angles: \citet{amonkar2025realitycheck} show that LLM-as-formalizer degrades with problem complexity on constraint satisfaction tasks, and NSVIF \citep{nsvif2026} demonstrates that neuro-symbolic verification of instruction following requires structured constraint solvers rather than LLM self-assessment. LogicReward \citep{xu2026logicreward} takes the opposite approach, training a reward model with theorem-prover supervision to improve step-level logical correctness, achieving gains that self-extraction cannot. Our finding that self-extracted constraints lack discriminative power complements these results: LLMs struggle both to solve formal constraints directly and to produce useful constraints from their own reasoning.

\paragraph{Debate and Multi-Agent Aggregation.}
Multi-agent debate refines answers through iterative natural language discussion among LLM instances \citep{liang2023encouraging,du2023improving}. In debate, models exchange arguments in plain text and update their positions based on peer responses. SICA takes a structurally different approach: instead of natural language refinement, it extracts formal constraints and resolves disagreements through satisfiability optimization. Both families attempt to move beyond simple voting, but debate preserves natural language flexibility while SICA commits to formal structure. \citet{huang2024selfcorrect} show that LLMs struggle to self-correct reasoning without external feedback, Self-Refine \citep{madaan2023selfrefine} demonstrates iterative refinement through self-feedback but without an external verifier the corrective signal remains bounded by the model's own assessment, and \citet{mirzadeh2024gsmsymbolic} demonstrate that LLM mathematical reasoning is fragile under symbolic perturbations. \citet{stechly2025selfverification} provide the most direct parallel: they show that LLM self-verification on reasoning and planning tasks degrades performance, while sound external verification yields significant gains. Their finding that verification is not inherently easier than generation for these models is structurally isomorphic to our capability trap, but operates in natural language self-critique rather than FOL+SAT. Where Stechly et al.\ establish a binary outcome (self-verification degrades performance), our work provides a different category of evidence: causal decomposition via oracle isolation, quantitative mechanism diagnosis through dual discriminativeness metrics, and a deployable screening criterion ($C_0$). The limitation persists even in a formal symbolic setting where constraints are syntactically valid FOL and processed by a sound solver, yet carry no discriminative signal beyond vote counting. \citet{valmeekam2023planning} and \citet{kambhampati2024position} further establish that LLMs lack autonomous planning ability and require external modules in ``LLM-Modulo'' frameworks to achieve reliable performance, reinforcing the pattern that self-reliance is the bottleneck. Across all six lines of work reviewed here, the consistent pattern is that improving on majority voting requires information beyond what the reasoning model can extract from its own outputs, whether from trained verifiers, external solvers, human annotations, or cross-model interaction.

